  "verbalization": "Lanostane is shown to interact with Carboxylesterase 2 through a series of molecular targets, with MIP, Anolobine, and Phlorigidoside C all acting as intermediates in this relationship. The final scores from the three paths suggest a consistent and plausible mechanistic link between Lanostane and Carboxylesterase 2, with MIP serving as a central node in the pathway. The evidence implies that Lanostane may inhibit Carboxylesterase 2 through a mechanism involving the binding of its structural analogs to the enzyme, potentially altering its activity or stability. The presence of multiple targets, all of which are known to interact with Carboxylesterase 2, supports the hypothesis that Lanostane exerts its effects via a shared biochemical pathway. The integration of these findings suggests that Lanostane may act as a competitive inhibitor of Carboxylesterase 2, a conclusion that is further supported by the consistent scores across the different paths.",
